          (resolution . "FIXED procedurally. A rebind now checks the finished records for any superseded head still named as a live claim, and reports it, so the next rebind fails loudly instead of silently. The check is scoped by key rather than by text: the review record's per-round reviewed-sha legitimately names historical heads, and a global substitution would reintroduce R6-F1 for the fifth time. A second procedural error is recorded because it nearly became a third: a chained string-replace in this remediation corrupted a field into a doubled SHA, caught only by re-reading the record. Set the key through the hash, not through text position - which is what the review record's own per-round heads have required since R6-F1.")
          (severity . "moderate")
          (summary . "Procedural: all four findings share one cause, and it is a gap in the REBIND PROCEDURE rather than in any record. Rebinding updated the binding fields (implementation-sha, content-digest) but not the prose naming the same head, so every rebind this wave created a new class of self-contradiction. Rounds 6, 7, 8 and 9 each found defects introduced by the previous round's remediation, which is a convergence failure worth naming rather than treating as unlucky.")
