      ))
      (note . "Round 10 ran to completion with a shell and, like round 9, executed all five checks rather than simulating them: the digest matched, record-commit returned pure, verify returned digest-ok, the gate reported exactly the two expected rejections with reject! confirmed to append rather than short-circuit, all three manifests verified (12/12, 6/6, 1/1), provenance passed with 950 notes, and lint-all reported 23 passed and the same 2 pre-existing warnings. Every numeric claim was re-derived independently and the round swept the superseded heads and the duplicate-key risk again; those held. Two findings, and both are the defect class this wave has produced most often: a live surface carrying a count that was true when last measured and was not re-measured when the data beneath it grew. Neither touches binding, digest, provenance or measurement, and the finding count fell from six to two, which is the first evidence of convergence in the review process rather than in the content - the content converged several rounds ago, and it is the prose and its aggregates that have not. The more interesting finding is the first, because its fix was not a number. The retained chain capture records 9/9 and 16/16 and cannot record anything else without re-running a 29-minute suite, so the honest repair is to state the current totals as measured now and to label the capture as a historical snapshot of its own run, rather than to quote it as current or to restate it into agreement. Both were available and both would have been false. Round 11 is pending.")
      (reviewed-sha . "b83d16f39ec1481612ca011a2572b2f3a541ae45")
      (reviewer . "zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash (independent, read-only, exec enabled)")
      (round . 10)
      (verdict . "CHANGES-REQUESTED")
    )
    #hasheq(
      (execution . "PARTIAL - all seven mandated execution checks NOT-RUN and reported as such; static analysis substituted and labelled; one of two findings real, one refuted")
      (findings . (
        #hasheq(
          (id . "W7-R11-F1")
          (resolution . "FIXED by re-measurement, and the fix is not a number correction. The count survived because 28f39f30d happens to sit on the b0f19d6bd..content segment, and that range is also sixteen paths, so a sentence that mixed three ranges and mislabelled one of them produced an arithmetically true figure. That is the most dangerous shape a record defect can take: it survives review by being true while being evidentially false, and four revisions of this field had each restated something wrong at the head carrying it. The field now measures each range separately - chain head to preflight commit is 5, preflight commit to content head is 18, chain head to content head is 19 - and names both stable anchors instead of reaching the chain head by pointing at the capture, which is how the two ranges came to be confused in the first place. It deliberately does not name the content head: the artifact is bound by SHA256SUMS, its digest feeds the evidence binding, and committing it moves any head it names, so it points at the evidence record's implementation-sha and merge-authorization.head as the authority instead. Both generators are bound in the manifest following the W6 precedent, so the correction is reproducible rather than asserted; the second is idempotent and keyed on shapes rather than digits, because restating the counts writes a generator into the manifest and moves the counts again.")
          (severity . "moderate")
          (status . "FIXED")
          (summary . "The preflight chain field conflated three commits as one. It claimed the sixteen-path enumeration was the delta between the commit named inside verify-chain.txt and this artifact's own commit field, and it named 28f39f30d as that endpoint. Both halves are false and wrong in the same direction: verify-chain.txt names only e9af72fee and cannot name the commit that carries it, b0f19d6bd is a DESCENDANT of that head rather than an ancestor, and the delta between them is 5 paths, not 16. The endpoint 28f39f30d was a third commit again - an intermediate on the way, the round-9 remediation.")
        )
        #hasheq(
          (id . "W7-R11-F2")
          (resolution . "NOT FIXED, because the finding is false, and acting on it would have corrupted a correct record. The W7 manifest holds 12 entries because it binds 9 files under raw/ and 3 that are not captures: release-preflight.json, w4-recovery.json and the release report. '(9 captures)' counts the captures and is correct; the reviewer counted 8 raw files, missed one, compared the capture count against the entry count, and concluded a correct figure was stale. The error is instructive rather than trivial: two different units - entries and captures - were read as one, which is a counting error of exactly the kind this wave has been about. Had the finding been acted on, '(9 captures)' would have become '(12 captures)' and been wrong twice over. The field was instead restated to the unambiguous form - entry counts with the raw count given alongside - so the ambiguity is gone without the figure being changed. Rejected findings are recorded here rather than dropped, because a review that only keeps what it got right teaches nothing about which checks are load-bearing.")
          (root-cause . "Two units read as one. The manifest binds twelve entries; nine of them are captures under raw/ and three are not. The reviewer counted eight raw files, then compared that capture count against the entry count. The field was never stale - the comparison was, and a unit error is the same class of thing this wave exists to police, which is why it is recorded here rather than merely dismissed.")
          (severity . "moderate")
          (status . "REFUTED - not a defect")
          (summary . "The round reported that the validation record's checksums field presents '(9 captures) ... all verify' in the present tense while the W7 manifest holds twelve entries, and called the 9 a superseded figure presented as current.")
        )
      ))
      (not-run . ("gsd-evidence-bind.rkt digest / record-commit / verify (the binding)" "git diff on the record commit (record-commit purity)" "gsd-wave-gate.rkt (the gate; static analysis of its preconditions substituted)" "sha256sum -c on all three manifests (the checksums; manifest contents verified instead)" "verify-artifact-provenance.rkt and lint-all.rkt" "git diff --name-only enumerations (the path counts)" "the review record's interior: round count, per-round findings sum, the historical " "reviewed-sha values, and any duplicate top-level key beyond character 500 - the file is " "one 62.2 KB line, the read tool refuses lines over 50 KB, and grep shows only the first " "500 characters, with no second copy in the tree"))
      (note . "Round 11 returned CHANGES-REQUESTED with two findings, and it is a different kind of round from the ten before it: the reviewer had no shell, said so plainly, and returned a NOT-RUN register naming all seven mandated checks it could not execute. That register is the most valuable part of the round. It refused to reason about whether the binding, gate and checksum commands would pass, it noticed that the review record is a single 62 KB line its tools cannot read past 500 characters, and it declined to substitute an in-tree claim for a measurement it had not made. What it could check from raw text, it checked, and one of the two findings it raised is real. The real one, F1, is the best example yet of this wave's central theme, and it is worth stating precisely because the defect was invisible to arithmetic. The field named three commits as one and produced the right number anyway, so it would have passed any check that counts paths. The evidence-only-record discipline, the stale-live-claim sweep and the gate would all have passed it. Only reading the sentence and asking which commit each range runs between found it, and the sentence it was found in had already survived four revisions. The second finding, F2, is refuted: the manifest's 12 entries are 9 captures plus 3 non-captures, the reviewer counted 8 raw files and compared captures to entries, and acting on it would have made a correct record wrong. Both facts are recorded. The finding count is 58 accepted out of 59 raised, and the distinction is the point: a review process that cannot reject its own findings is not converging, it is accumulating. The convergence evidence is that the real finding was a prose defect in an artifact rather than a defect in a binding, a digest, a measurement or the content, and that the fix is a re-measurement with the generators bound so it is reproducible. The standing problem is unchanged and is now structural rather than incidental: the mandated review route has lost its exec four rounds running, and every check that has to be executed - the binding, the gate, the checksums, the provenance, the lint - is therefore unverified by the reviewer at exactly the point where it matters most. Eleven rounds of reading have found real defects, and reading is not what is failing. Round 12 is pending and must have a shell.")
      (reviewed-sha . "856f485098c3ccd3cf9e9e6d856e747bf5d241a6")
      (reviewer . "zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash (independent, read-only; NO exec - self-reported)")
      (round . 11)
      (verdict . "CHANGES-REQUESTED")
    )
    #hasheq(
      (execution . "BLOCKED - all seven mandated execution checks refused by the session; marked BLOCKED rather than inferred; gate preconditions analysed statically and labelled as not a run")
      (findings . (
        #hasheq(
          (id . "W7-R12-F1")
          (resolution . "NOT FIXED in the tree, because it is not in the tree. This is the sole reason the wave is not merge-authorized, and it is not a wave-content defect: the binding, the gate, the checksums, the provenance and the lint have all been run by the implementer and pass, and the round that matters is the one that certifies them independently. Two mitigations are in place. The reviewer's static analysis of the gate's preconditions is recorded as corroboration, clearly labelled as not a run, and it found no third rejection. And the record now carries the NOT-RUN register as a first-class list, so a round that cannot execute says exactly which checks it skipped rather than leaving the omission to be inferred from a verdict.")
          (root-cause . "The mandated review route is not receiving a shell. Two attempts this round and one the round before granted an exec allowlist, and the reviewer reported no exec tool in the session in all three cases. Rounds 9 and 10 did execute their checks, so the capability existed and has since been lost on the route rather than absent from the harness.")
          (severity . "blocking")
          (status . "OPEN - infrastructure, not wave content")
          (summary . "All seven mandated execution checks BLOCKED: the evidence binding, the record-commit purity diff, the gate run, sha256sum -c on all three manifests, artifact provenance, lint-all, and the git path enumerations. The reviewer refused to issue APPROVED over checks it had not run and returned CHANGES-REQUESTED on that ground alone, which is the first time a round in this wave has declined to approve for want of evidence rather than for a defect it had found.")
        )
        #hasheq(
          (id . "W7-R12-F2")
          (resolution . "FIXED, and it is the most consequential change this wave has made to its own instruments. All three records are now laid out over 36, 473 and 111 lines with a longest line of 5773 characters, so the whole of each is openable and the tally is independently checkable: 4+4+6+9+9+7+7+5+4+2+2 = 59 across eleven rounds, one marked REFUTED, giving the 58 accepted the verdict claims. Every per-round reviewed-sha is readable, including the legitimate duplicate shared by rounds 9 and 10. Only layout changed - no value moved, the content digest is byte-identical, and the gate still reports exactly the two honest rejections. Two dead ends are recorded in the commit rather than hidden: racket/pretty's pretty-print emitted a leading quote so the result no longer read back as a hash, and equal? is unusable on these records because they mix one immutable top-level hash with 71 mutable nested ones and equal? is #f for a mutable and an immutable hash of identical contents. The check used instead is mutability-independent structural equality plus layout idempotence.")
          (root-cause . "The records were written as a single datum on one line, so a reviewer whose read tool caps lines at 50 KB could not open the 71 KB review record at all, and grep showed only the first 500 characters of any match. The record was therefore unreviewable in the one place where review is the point.")
          (severity . "moderate")
          (status . "FIXED")
          (summary . "docs/reports/gsd-wave-reviews/v1.00.31-w7.rktd is a single 71 KB line, exceeding the session read tool's 50 KB per-line cap, and grep displays only the first 500 characters of a match. The rounds array, every per-round findings array, the verdict and the historical reviewed-sha values were consequently unverifiable. The round demonstrated the cost rather than asserting it: asked to check that the aggregate was 58 accepted and 1 refuted, it reported that the word 'refuted' and the id W7-R11-F2 were ABSENT from a record that contains both, because its probes could only see the first 500 characters.")
        )
      ))
      (not-run . ("gsd-evidence-bind.rkt digest / record-commit / verify (the binding)" "git diff on the record commit (record-commit purity)" "gsd-wave-gate.rkt (the gate; static precondition analysis substituted, labelled)" "sha256sum -c on all three manifests (contents counted, digests not computed)" "verify-artifact-provenance.rkt and lint-all.rkt" "git diff --name-only enumerations (the 18 / 19 / 5 path counts)" "the review record's interior - the finding aggregate, the historical reviewed-sha values, and the duplicate-key scan - because the file was one 71 KB line, the read tool refuses lines over 50 KB, and grep showed only the first 500 characters"))
      (note . "Round 12 is the round that explains the other eleven. It found no defect in the wave content, invented none, and returned CHANGES-REQUESTED anyway - on the ground that it could not execute the checks it was commissioned to run. It was granted an exec allowlist, found none in its session, marked all seven mandated checks BLOCKED rather than reasoning about whether they would pass, and declined to issue an approval it could not stand behind. That is the correct behaviour and it is worth being explicit about what it means for this wave: after twelve rounds, the obstacle to merge is not the content and not the prose, both of which have converged, and not the instruments, all of which pass when the implementer runs them. It is that the one party whose job is independent certification has not been able to run anything for four consecutive rounds. The round's second finding is the one that should worry a future session most, because it was nearly an approval by omission. The review record had grown to a single 71 KB line that the reviewer's read tool refuses and grep truncates to 500 characters, so the finding arrays it was asked to sum were invisible. Its probes over the first 500 characters returned results consistent with the claims and proved nothing - it reported the word 'refuted' and the id W7-R11-F2 as absent from a record containing both. Had the round been less careful it would have certified an aggregate it never saw, and the wave would have merged on that. The records are now openable end to end, which is the precondition for every future round being worth anything. The pattern across all twelve rounds is that the defects have moved steadily upward: first instrument failures that permitted vacuous greens, then evidence and binding errors, then prose that no gate reads, then a derived aggregate mislabelled while arithmetically true, and now the instruments' own readability. Each layer had to be fixed before the next became visible, and the current layer is the one that makes the others auditable. That is progress, and it is not the same as being close. Round 13 is pending and must have a shell.")
      (reviewed-sha . "98e582e019277046290f67a6207d8555d5442041")
      (reviewer . "zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash (independent, read-only; exec allowlist granted and NOT present)")
      (round . 12)
      (verdict . "CHANGES-REQUESTED")
    )
    #hasheq(
      (execution . "COMPLETE - every mandated check executed; digest matched, record-commit pure, verify digest-ok, gate exactly two rejections, 21/21 checksums, provenance 950 notes, lint 23 passed / 2 pre-existing warnings")
      (findings . (
        #hasheq(
          (id . "W7-R13-F1")
          (resolution . "FIXED at the writer, not in the text. All four figures are now computed from the per-round findings arrays when the verdict is built, and an assertion requires them to partition the total - which is the property nothing was checking, and the reason the error survived while every other figure in the record was correct. The verdict also now states its counting convention outright, because 61 is the array total and R4-F7 is a real finding recorded outside round 4's array, so a reader comparing the total against the wave's finding history would otherwise be misled by a number that was never wrong.")
          (root-cause . "A derived aggregate retyped by hand. The verdict's three figures did not partition: 58 accepted + 1 refuted + 1 open = 60 against 61 findings in the arrays. The 58 was correct for the eleven-round state and was left in place when round 12 added one accepted and one open finding. This is the same defect the record was corrected for once already - a hand-maintained total wrong by one for four rounds - reintroduced by the author of that correction, because the rule lived in prose rather than in the thing that writes the field. A rule not enforced by the writer is not a rule.")
          (severity . "moderate")
          (status . "FIXED")
          (summary . "The top-level verdict read '61 findings raised, 58 accepted after one was refuted, 1 still open'. Round 13 executed the checks and derived the dispositions from the arrays itself: 61 total, 1 refuted, 1 open, therefore 59 accepted. 58 + 1 + 1 = 60, not 61. The figure is one stale generation behind.")
        )
        #hasheq(
          (id . "W7-R13-F2")
          (resolution . "REMOVED, not annotated. Annotating a duplicate leaves two copies of a paragraph in a record whose whole argument is that each claim should be stated once and should not fall behind, and one of the two would still be the stale copy. The fix script locates the second copy, asserts its distinctive tail already appears earlier in the field - so it is provably the duplicate and not a distinct passage - and only then removes it. The field drops from 5760 to 5127 characters and 'This review is the eighth' now occurs zero times.")
          (root-cause . "A remediation that appended instead of replaced, leaving a byte-identical copy of a paragraph behind. The duplicate is also four generations stale, opening 'This review is the eighth' in a record at twelve rounds.")
          (severity . "moderate")
          (status . "FIXED")
          (summary . "The report field contains one paragraph twice, verbatim. Round 13 saw the stale deixis and classified it as covered by the record's own design statement rather than as a defect. Verifying it showed the second occurrence is a byte-identical duplicate of the preceding paragraph, from 4317 to 4950 of the field - 633 characters - so it is a residue of a remediation that appended rather than replaced, not merely a number that fell behind.")
        )
      ))
      (not-run . ("the fast test suite - not in this round's mandate; lint-all's tests check and " "the bound fast-suite capture cover it"))
      (note . "Round 13 had a shell, and that changes what this wave can say. Twelve rounds have returned CHANGES-REQUESTED, and rounds 1-8, 11 and 12 did so for two different reasons: rounds 1-8 found real defects, and rounds 11 and 12 found none they could certify because they could not execute anything. Round 13 executed all eight mandated checks and all ten re-derivations, pasted the real output for each, and every one held: the digest, record-commit purity, the verify, the gate reporting exactly the two honest rejections and no third, all twenty-one checksums across three manifests, provenance, lint at 23 passed and the same two pre-existing warnings, all three path enumerations at 5, 18 and 19, zero duplicate top-level keys in any record, the live-versus-historical classification, count hygiene on both live surfaces, the chain-field repair against its own measurements, the gate's preconditions, and the record layouts. The four-round outage in W7-R12-F1 is resolved and is recorded as resolved. It found two defects, both in this record, and neither touches binding, digest, checksum, provenance, measurement or the W7 content. The first is its own accepted-count being stale by one, and the reason is the most uncomfortable finding of the wave: the aggregate had already been corrected once for being hand-maintained, the correction made it derived, and the very next round typed the figure back into a format string. A rule enforced only in prose does not survive its own author. The fix moves the arithmetic into the writer and adds the assertion that was missing - that the three dispositions must partition the total - which is the property that would have caught it and which nothing was checking. The second is a duplicated paragraph I found while verifying the first, which round 13 had classified as context rather than a defect; it is a byte-identical copy left by a remediation that appended instead of replacing. It is removed rather than annotated. What the wave has established across thirteen rounds is that the W7 content is sound by every check that can be run, and that the remaining surface is the record describing itself. That is a real convergence, and it is narrower than it looks: the last four findings were all in prose, and prose is exactly what no gate in this repository reads, so a wave can be made of them indefinitely. The difference now is that a round with a shell can see them, which four rounds ago it could not. Round 14 is pending.")
      (reviewed-sha . "98e582e019277046290f67a6207d8555d5442041")
      (reviewer . "zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash (independent, read-only; EXEC PRESENT - all eight mandated checks and all ten re-derivations executed with real output, ending the four-round outage recorded as W7-R12-F1)")
      (round . 13)
      (verdict . "CHANGES-REQUESTED")
    )
    #hasheq(
      (execution . "PARTIAL - eight of nine mandated gates executed. digest matched; record-commit pure; verify digest-ok; gate reported exactly the two expected rejections and no third; W7 checksums 14/14; W0 checksums 1/1; provenance ok; lint 23 passed / 2 pre-existing warnings. The ninth, the W6 checksum, could not be executed as mandated because the instruction named a manifest that does not exist - see W7-R14-F1. The reviewer located the real W6 manifest and verified 6/6 itself.")
      (findings . (
        #hasheq(
          (id . "W7-R14-F1")
          (resolution . "Corrected in the round 15 instruction, and the corrected path is verified to exist and to pass 6/6 from the repository root before being handed to a reviewer. Recorded against the instruction rather than dropped, because a mandated check that cannot be executed is a missing gate and this wave has twice been damaged by exactly that: W7-R12-F1 and the bare-raco-test sweep that exited 0 without running anything.")
          (root-cause . "A wrong path in the review instruction I wrote, not a defect in any artifact. There is exactly one tier-ownership manifest in the tree, artifacts/tier-ownership/v1.00.31-w0/SHA256SUMS; the W6 manifest is under wave-delivery-integrity. No file in the repository is wrong.")
          (severity . "low")
          (status . "FIXED AT SOURCE - in the review instruction, not in the tree")
          (summary . "The mandated checksum command for W6 named artifacts/tier-ownership/v1.00.31-w6/SHA256SUMS, which does not exist. sha256sum exits 1 with 'Datei oder Verzeichnis nicht gefunden', so a mandated check could not be executed as written. The reviewer reported it rather than silently substituting the manifest it found, which is the correct behaviour and is why it surfaced at all.")
        )
        #hasheq(
          (found-by . "NOT raised by the round 14 reviewer. Found by me while writing the classifier that computes this record's disposition counts, immediately after round 14 returned. It is recorded here because it was found while adjudicating round 14, not attributed to a review that did not raise it.")
          (id . "W7-R14-F2")
          (resolution . "The 57 findings are NOT backfilled with a status, because assigning 57 dispositions from prose at volume is the very move this wave exists to stop: it would manufacture exactly the record the verdict was pretending to have. Instead the classifier loses its default branch, so a finding with no status is unclassified and is counted as such, and the verdict now states the real shape of the data: the total, how many findings carry an explicit status, and how many carry none. The partition assertion is gone, because there is no partition to assert over unrecorded dispositions. Rounds 11 onward do record a status on every finding, so the record is well-formed going forward and the deficit is historical, confined to rounds 1-10, and permanent as history.")
          (root-cause . "A classifier with a default branch, used as a measurement instrument. The missing value and the meaningful value were both 'accepted', so the error was invisible to every check: the count was self-consistent, the partition assertion passed, and a reviewer reading the verdict would see four numbers that added up. This is the same failure as W7-R13-F1 one level deeper. F1 was a derived total that went stale by one generation; this is a derived category that never existed in the data. The wave had spent four rounds hardening the arithmetic of a number whose categories were being invented as it was written.")
          (severity . "high")
          (status . "FIXED - the unsupported claim is withdrawn, not backfilled")
          (summary . "The verdict asserts a four-way disposition partition over all 63 findings - '61 accepted, 1 refuted, 1 still open' - but only 6 of the 63 carry a status at all. Rounds 1 through 10 wrote findings with exactly the keys id, resolution, severity and summary, and no status, so 57 findings have no recorded disposition. The word 'accepted' for those 57 was supplied by the classifier's default branch at write time. The verdict was reporting an inference as though it were a recorded fact, and round 13's assertion that the dispositions partition the total was checking that inference against itself.")
        )
        #hasheq(
          (found-by . "NOT raised by the round 14 reviewer. Found by me while running the script that writes this entry, before it wrote anything.")
          (id . "W7-R14-F3")
          (resolution . "Separators are whitespace, and the idempotence assertion stays and is now load-bearing rather than decorative. The first version of the script also had one paren short in the verdict's hash-set, and the balance guard caught it before the file was written. Three defects in one remediation tool, none of them visible to the artefact they were repairing.")
          (root-cause . "The emitter was written from memory of a hash syntax that is not Racket's, and the failure mode is the dangerous one: a reader that rejects bad input would have surfaced this immediately, but ',' is valid Racket with different meaning, so the corruption passed every check that only asked whether the text parsed. It was caught solely by the assertion that re-emitting a rendered record is a structural no-op - an assertion I nearly dropped as ceremonial when porting the emitter, and which is the only reason the record was not written corrupt.")
          (severity . "high")
          (status . "FIXED - in the remediation tool, before it could write the record")
          (summary . "The hand-rolled record emitter written for this round separated hash-pair clauses and list elements with a comma. Racket's reader does not accept a comma as a separator there: it reads ',' as UNQUOTE. So the emitter produced text that looked like a hash and parsed as something else, and it did so silently - no error, no warning, a datum of the right shape and the wrong contents. The two renderings differed by 4278 characters.")
        )
      ))
      (not-run . ("sha256sum -c artifacts/tier-ownership/v1.00.31-w6/SHA256SUMS - not run as mandated: no such file. The reviewer substituted the correct W6 manifest and reported 6/6 OK." "the fast test suite - not in this round's mandate; lint-all's tests check and " "the bound fast-suite capture cover it"))
      (note . "Round 14 executed nearly everything, found almost nothing in the content, and returned one finding aimed at the review process rather than the tree. Adjudicating it turned up the deepest defect in the wave, and the two are related in a way worth stating. The reviewer's finding was that a path in my instruction did not exist, so a mandated check could not run. Mine is that a category in my verdict did not exist either, so a mandated assertion - that the dispositions partition the total - was passing while measuring nothing. In both cases a value that should have come from the data was supplied by me at write time, and in both cases the error was invisible to the arithmetic because I was the one supplying the number the arithmetic was checked against. An instrument that shares an author with the thing it measures will agree with it. On the content: fourteen rounds have now run, and the last three found nothing in the W7 content, in the binding, the digest, the checksums, the provenance or the measurement. Every remaining defect is in the record describing itself, and the record is prose, and no gate in this repository reads prose - which means a wave can be made of these indefinitely. Round 13 was the first round with a shell and it found four; round 14 had one and found one, and the one it found was in the harness. That is progress, and it is progress in the review rather than in the product, which is the honest shape of this wave at fourteen rounds. Two process notes. This round ran on gpt-5.5, not the mandated zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash, because I launched it without the model override and the child inherited the session default. It is recorded as advisory and does not count toward approval, whatever it says; the route constraint is one of the few things holding this process independent, and I weakened it by omission rather than by decision, which is worse than the reverse. And the reviewer's provenance figure of 952 notes appeared to contradict this record's live claim of 951 for w6 and 950 for w7; I took that for a stale live claim of the class this wave exists to police, and I was one reproduction away from recording a fifteenth false finding. Both figures are correct: 952 is the default whole-repository invocation, 950 is --current-wave v1.00.31-w7, 951 is --current-wave v1.00.31-w6. They measure different things and neither is stale. The rule that saved it is the one this wave has been paying for the hard way - reproduce a finding before it becomes a finding - and the corollary is that the same rule applies to the tools I write: my own classifier is an instrument, and it needed the same scrutiny as the reviewer's shell. Round 15 is pending and must run on the mandated route.")
      (reviewed-sha . "98e582e019277046290f67a6207d8555d5442041")
      (reviewer . "gpt-5.5 (independent, read-only; EXEC PRESENT - eight of the nine mandated gates executed with real pasted output, and every re-derivation ran). NOT the mandated independent route: this round was launched without the model override and ran on the session default. The route constraint names zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash, so this round is recorded for completeness and advisory value and does NOT count as the round that can authorize approval. Its finding stands regardless of model, because it is a checkable fact about a path rather than a judgement.")
      (round . 14)
      (verdict . "CHANGES-REQUESTED")
    )
  ))
  (schema-version . 2)
  (scope . "W7 blocked-wave repair, bake and v1.00.31 release, reviewed read-only against the frozen W7 wave doc in the campaign plan snapshot. Rounds 1-4 covered the runner base-dir fix and its regression tests, the three classes of version-coincidence defect, the W2 gate-integrity repair and its red-first proof, the W6 rehearsal matrix regeneration and head re-stamp, the W4 local-capture correction, the release honesty of the CHANGELOG entry and the release report, and the coherence and substantiation of the W7 evidence captures. Each round was a fresh read-only reviewer at the then-current head; no finding was accepted without being reproduced against the tree first.")
  (timestamp . "2026-09-25T17:10:00Z (r1) / 2026-09-25T17:35:00Z (r2) / 2026-09-25T20:20:00Z (r3) / 2026-09-25T22:30:00Z (r4) / 2026-09-26T02:05:00Z (r5, zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash) / 2026-09-26T04:20:00Z (r6, zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash) / 2026-09-26T05:35:00Z (r7, zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash) / 2026-09-26T06:49:10Z (r8, zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash) / 2026-09-26T12:17:17Z (r9, zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash, exec enabled) / 2026-09-26T13:15:00Z (r10, zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash, exec enabled) / 2026-09-26T22:40:00Z (r11, zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash, no exec - NOT-RUN register returned) / 2026-09-27T01:40:00Z (r12, zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash, exec allowlist granted but not present - all seven checks BLOCKED) / 2026-09-27T02:15:00Z (r13, zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash, EXEC PRESENT - all mandated checks executed, outage resolved) / 2026-09-27T02:50:00Z (r14, gpt-5.5, EXEC PRESENT - eight of nine mandated gates executed; NOT the mandated route, so advisory and not counted toward approval)")
  (tooling-notes . "Round 3 was obtained on a later attempt after three provider failures: a usage-limit error on the reviewer role's default model, an OpenRouter credit limit (402) on an alternative, and an OpenRouter monthly key limit (403) on a third. Round 5 was first attempted on the routes that had served rounds 3 and 4 and could not finish: all four configured providers were quota-exhausted, the last returning a five-hour usage limit partway through. That attempt produced no verdict and is recorded as such. Round 5 was subsequently run to completion through zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash and returned CHANGES-REQUESTED, with every finding it raised fixed, as was round 6. The earlier wording here said round 5 could not run without qualification, which stopped being true when that round completed; the failed attempt and the completed round are now distinguished rather than conflated. No verdict was invented in place of any of these failures. The pattern across all of them is the same and is worth stating plainly: a review that cannot run is a missing gate, never an implicit approval, and the evidence record says so in its status field.")
  (verdict . "CHANGES-REQUESTED (rounds 1-14; 66 findings raised across the per-round findings arrays. 9 of them carry an explicit machine-readable status - 7 FIXED, 1 REFUTED, 1 OPEN - and the remaining 57 carry no status at all: rounds 1-10 wrote findings with only id, resolution, severity and summary, so for those the record asserts NO disposition and the number is not an inference presented as a fact. The old verdict counted all 66 as 'accepted'; that was a classifier default, not a recorded value, and it is withdrawn rather than backfilled - see W7-R14-F2. Every finding from round 11 onward does carry a status, so the record is well-formed going forward. Round 15 PENDING - NOT APPROVED, and this record must not be read as approval. The total is over the per-round findings arrays, the record's declared basis; R4-F7 is a real finding recorded separately in round 4 and is not in those arrays, so this total is the array total and not the wave's finding count. Round 14 ran on gpt-5.5 rather than the mandated independent route and is advisory; approval requires a round on the mandated route.")
)
